{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Strong upward-trend underestimation: when the last 5–10 days show a clear rally (higher highs / higher lows), the model is consistently too low across the whole 30d horizon. Apply an additive uplift that grows with horizon (small in days 1–3, larger by days 10–30).",
    "Horizon-dependent bias: errors increase with lead time. Use a ramp correction (e.g., +0.5 to +1.5 EUR in week 1, +3 to +6 EUR by weeks 2–4) rather than a flat shift.",
    "Lag/slow trend propagation: the forecast path often stays near the recent range while the realized series continues trending. Increase forecast slope (trend term) when recent momentum is positive; do not anchor to the 30d mean in that regime.",
    "Over-mean-reversion after breakouts: after a breakout to new local highs, the model predicts pullbacks/sideways, but realized prices keep grinding higher. Suppress mean-reversion strength for 2–3 weeks following a regime shift (new 30d high).",
    "Volatility compression: the model’s forecast range is too narrow versus realized moves during rallies. Inflate volatility/dispersion (wider upward excursions) when recent realized volatility is rising alongside positive momentum."
  ]
}
